<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

/*
|==============================================================================
| Velu Hive -- standalone agent client
|==============================================================================
|
| ONE FILE. NO DEPENDENCIES. NO COMPOSER. NO AUTOLOADER.
|
| Lives on each developer's machine at ~/.claude/hive/hive.php and is invoked by
| Claude Code hooks, in exec form (`command` <php>, `args`
| [<home>/.claude/hive/hive.php, <verb>]), which Claude Code runs with no
| shell at all, so one hook works under Git Bash and PowerShell alike: a
| quoted "<php>" "<client>" string is a parse error in the PowerShell Claude
| Code falls back to without Git Bash (AUD-33).
|
| <php> is the absolute path of the interpreter `hive.php setup` ran on, never
| a bare `php`: the PATH Claude Code starts hooks with may have no php on it,
| and many machines have none at all. The installer the one-paste prompt from
| /board -> My access runs (/install/setup.sh, setup.ps1) finds a PHP 8.1 or
| newer, or brings its own into ~/.claude/hive/php, and runs setup with it.
| Every command this client tells a person or the agent to run names that
| same interpreter (hive_self_command()).
|
| PHP and not bash: bash would need `jq` on macOS plus a parallel .ps1 for
| Windows, and Node is no longer guaranteed now that Claude Code ships native
| binaries.
|
|------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| THE PRIVACY CONTRACT -- read this before changing anything
|------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| This client exists SOLELY because the zero-install alternative is disqualified.
| An `http`-type hook pointed straight at Hive POSTs the hook's ENTIRE stdin, and
| for an Edit that stdin contains old_string/new_string: literal source code from
| a private repository. There is no way to filter it.
|
| So this file is a mandatory allowlist filter. The rules, in order of how badly
| a violation would hurt:
|
|   1. Hook stdin is parsed ONCE, by hive_hook_input(), which returns ONLY the
|      handful of scalar fields named in HOOK_INPUT_ALLOWLIST. The decoded blob
|      is unset immediately. There is no variable anywhere else in this file
|      holding raw stdin, so there is no code path from hook stdin to the
|      network or to disk, but the ones rules 1a and 1b name. The status
|      line's envelope adds three, each spelled out there: workspace.current_dir,
|      model.id and, since 1.17.0, cost.total_duration_ms as a whole number,
|      which only the plugin repair reads and nothing sends.
|  1a. The work plan, since 1.10.0: an exception to rule 1, field by
|      field. Only for the verbs capture-plan and capture-steps, on a
|      PostToolUse whose tool_name is one of HIVE_PLAN_TOOLS, does
|      hive_hook_input() copy these, each spelled out in
|      hive_hook_plan_fields(), into a static of their own
|      (hive_hook_plan_input()), never into the scalar map above:
|        ExitPlanMode  tool_response.plan (a plan over HIVE_PLAN_READ_MAX
|                      bytes keeps only its size and SHA-256) and
|                      tool_response.planWasEdited
|        TaskCreate    tool_input.subject, tool_input.activeForm (or
|                      active_form), and the new task's id from
|                      tool_response.task.id (or tool_response.id)
|        TaskUpdate    tool_input.taskId (or id, task_id, taskid: Claude Code
|                      repairs that key), status, subject, activeForm (or
|                      active_form)
|        TodoWrite     tool_input.todos[i].content, .status and .activeForm,
|                      for the first HIVE_PLAN_STEPS_MAX, and how many there are
|        all four      whether agent_id is present, never its value
|      Never read: tool_input.plan, planFilePath, tool_response.filePath,
|      allowedPrompts, requestId, awaitingLeaderApproval, a task's
|      description, metadata, owner, addBlocks and addBlockedBy,
|      tool_response.oldTodos and newTodos, transcript_path,
|      agent_transcript_path, last_assistant_message, the plan file, and
|      ~/.claude/plans or ~/.claude/tasks. What is read goes into the local
|      spool alone (SECTION 18d), and from there, as a step's words or a
|      plan's text, to PUT /progress/runs, which only the developer and
|      Velu's owners can read back. A step or plan shaped like a credential,
|      or holding a control, bidi or zero-width character, is held back
|      whole, never cleaned. The progress verbs read one environment
|      variable for it, CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID, and keep only its digest
|      (rule 2).
|  1b. The kind of a shell command, since 1.11.0: the other exception to
|      rule 1. Only for mark-dirty, on a PostToolUse whose tool is a shell
|      tool (Bash, PowerShell: `shell` in HIVE_ACTIVITY_TOOL_KINDS), does
|      hive_hook_input() read tool_input.command, and only to hand it to
|      hive_activity_shell_kind(), which matches the program and subcommand
|      words of each command in it against a fixed list -- git commit, git
|      push, gh pr create, edit, merge or ready, gh pr review, a test runner,
|      a build -- and answers one of HIVE_ACTIVITY_SHELL_KINDS, or `shell`.
|      The kind is all that is kept (`shell_kind`), and it leaves the machine
|      only as a count. Since hunt 7 (K5) the command is also handed to
|      hive_shell_places(), which reads the folders it works in (`cd X`,
|      `git -C X`), where it writes and its operands, as text (a Windows
|      backslash read as a slash), into a static of their own
|      (hive_hook_shell_places()) that mark-dirty compares with the
|      checkouts it knows, to mark only those the command names
|      (hive_shell_roots()); the places go no further than that comparison.
|      None reaches a filesystem call, a process or the network. The
|      command, its arguments, a commit message typed on it and its output
|      are never kept, written or sent.
|   2. Every outbound request body is built field by field by the verb that
|      sends it. Nothing is ever spread, merged or passed through from input.
|      Three hook fields shape a request without being copied into it:
|      SessionStart's `source` and SessionEnd's `reason` are mapped onto this
|      file's own literals (hive_enum), and the session id travels only as
|      `client_session`, the first 32 hex chars of its SHA-256, so the server
|      can tell a resumed conversation from a second terminal. Since 1.11.0
|      a sub-agent's agent_id travels only as its digest, the
|      first 32 hex characters of its SHA-256, as the session id does, and
|      its agent_type and the session's model only when they pass their
|      grammar (HIVE_AGENT_TYPE_PATTERN, HIVE_MODEL_PATTERN), else not at all.
|      Since 1.12.0 a session's label, which its developer types on `progress
|      session`, is sent only when it passes HIVE_SESSION_LABEL_PATTERN, to
|      PUT /progress/sessions/{digest}/label, and kept in lanes.json; a
|      handover names its target session the same way. Since 1.14.0 an
|      agent's one-line answer to its developer, typed on `progress say`, is
|      sent only when it passes the client's twin of RoomText
|      (hive_prompt_text_problem()), to POST /progress/says, and kept nowhere
|      here; a note its developer wrote it on Hive's board is taken by the
|      synchronous Stop or SessionStart alone, printed once, and kept
|      nowhere either (SECTION 34).
|   3. File CONTENTS, diffs, prompts, transcripts and commit messages are never
|      read. This client never opens a file inside the repository except .git
|      presence probes and an optional .hiveignore. A commit message typed on
|      a `git commit` command line is never kept: rule 1b keeps its kind.
*/
// (Rule 3a is written in line comments: a path glob it names would end a
// block comment.)
//  3a. Plan documents, since 1.11.0: the one exception to rule 3. The client
//      reads (i) a file the agent itself just wrote with Write, Edit or
//      NotebookEdit, when its path matches HIVE_PLAN_FILE_GLOBS inside the
//      workspace root or a tracked repository, and (ii) at session start, the
//      blobs of docs/superpowers/**/PROGRESS.md, and of the plan files the
//      server asks for, from the workspace's remote default branch as last
//      fetched, with git ls-tree and git cat-file. A file read under (i) must be
//      a regular file with no symbolic link on its path. Every document must be
//      at most HIVE_PLAN_DOC_MAX bytes, valid UTF-8, and carry a plan marker. A
//      credential, control, bidi or zero-width shape anywhere withholds the whole
//      document. What is read goes to a 0600 spool and to PUT
//      /progress/workplans/{source}, and nowhere else, and it is deleted once
//      Hive holds it. Only its size and SHA-256 reach the audit log. The owner
//      switch workplans.capture_files and a developer's HIVE_PLAN_FILES=0 turn
//      it off. Source code, diffs, prompts, transcripts and commit messages are
//      still never read.
/*
|   4. Paths matching a secret shape are DROPPED, not masked, and only a
|      redacted_count is reported. "secrets/<redacted>" still tells a reader
|      that Velu keeps per-client keys, which is exactly the leak we are
|      avoiding.
|   5. UserPromptSubmit is never registered as a hook at all.
|   6. Outside HIVE_REPO_ALLOWLIST the hooks exit having made no network call
|      and having written nothing at all -- not even a log line. Open a client
|      repo or a personal side project and this client is inert.
|   7. Token usage, since 1.15.0. When the owner switches usage on, Claude
|      Code's own metrics exporter sends token counts and model names for
|      every Claude Code session on this machine to Hive, once a minute.
|      Hive keeps only sessions in velu-media repositories and only counts:
|      never prompts, code, file names, paths or your email, which arrives
|      with each export and is discarded before anything is stored.
|      `hive.php usage off` stops it; `hive.php usage status` shows what is
|      set. This client sends nothing new itself and reads no export: setup
|      writes the exporter's metrics settings (HIVE_USAGE_ENV, never logs or
|      traces) and its otelHeadersHelper, `usage-headers`, which prints this
|      machine's usage token only to Claude Code, and the off header instead
|      in a folder outside HIVE_REPO_ALLOWLIST or while Hive or usage is off
|      here, which Hive refuses unread (SECTION 40).
|
| DELIBERATELY NOT IMPLEMENTED: scraping an intent line out of the transcript.
| The transcript is a prompt log; rule 3 outranks the convenience. Intent comes
| from HIVE_INTENT, --intent=, or a .hive-intent file -- all three explicit acts
| by the developer.
|
|------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| FAIL-OPEN, ALWAYS
|------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| A coordination tool that stops people working when the server is down gets
| uninstalled in week two. Every verb is wrapped so that any error, exception or
| fatal produces exit 0 and an empty stdout. The pre-edit gate in particular
| makes zero network calls and can only ever ADD a prompt, with one exception
| since 1.11.0 (decision D101): under autonomy auto with the owners' gate on
| deny, it refuses an edit of a file a live ruling says this agent yields, when
| all seven of its conditions hold (SECTION 32). Even then it fails open on
| every error, a cold or stale cache, a pause or enforce off. Two other places
| do not fail open: the lease (SECTION 31, decision D97) stops the START of a
| lane task Hive has not confirmed is still this session's. Since 1.13.0 the
| automation lease (`progress automation lease`) does the same for a step a
| developer's standing choice asked of their agent -- opening, readying or
| merging a pull request -- which runs only once Hive confirms it still
| stands. Each only stops a start: it never blocks an edit.
|
|------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| TEXT FROM THE SERVER IS DATA, NEVER INSTRUCTIONS
|------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Other developers' names, branches, titles, intents and paths reach this
| client through the server, and any teammate (or a compromised agent holding
| a token) chooses them. They end up in front of THIS developer's model: the
| <hive> block, the pre-edit prompt, the rewake text. So every such string goes
| through hive_safe_text() on the way in: < and > escaped, control and format
| characters (bidi overrides, zero-width, line separators) removed, whitespace
| collapsed to one line, length bounded. Nothing server-supplied can close the
| <hive> block, start a new line inside it, or reorder what a human reads.
| The one text kept on several lines is the owners' description of a backlog
| item `progress take` shows (hive_brief_lines()): each line made safe the
| same way but for its spacing, inside a <hive-brief> block nothing in it
| can close, and labelled as the owners' description of the work, data.
| Since 1.14.0 hive_safe_text() also replaces the developer block's trusted
| marks with [removed], so no teammate's string reads as that block, which
| is the one thing a hook prints that is not data: the developer's own
| nudge or note to their own session, <hive-from-your-developer>, first in
| the hook's output and only from their own take (SECTION 34).
|
| HIVE_URL gets the same rule everywhere that `setup` applies to --url: https,
| or plain http to a local development server only. The token is never sent
| anywhere else.
|
|------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| BRANCHES: WHERE THE WORK LANDS
|------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| A claim carries target_branch (feat/x -> dev: where the work will be merged)
| so the server can tell two people heading for different integration branches
| apart. It is NEVER guessed from the repository default. Resolution, first
| match wins: HIVE_TARGET_BRANCH, then `git config branch.<b>.hive-target`,
| then the branch's upstream when its name differs, then the branch this
| session just switched from when it is an ancestor of HEAD, then the nearest
| commit behind HEAD decorated with another branch, else null ("not said").
| Whatever is found is written back to `git config branch.<b>.hive-target`, so
| it survives sessions and a developer can correct it with one command.
|
| A mid-session switch to a branch cut from the previous one sends
| `supersedes` so the server closes the previous claim in the same write. Any
| other switch leaves the old claim alone: its unpushed work is still here.
|
|------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| WORKSPACES: ONE SESSION, SEVERAL REPOSITORIES
|------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Developers open Claude Code in velu-workspace, a git repository whose
| .gitignore excludes each velu-* clone inside it. Every clone is its own
| repository, so a session is bound not to ONE repo but to every repo it can
| touch: the one it starts in plus the git repositories directly inside it
| (their slugs read from each .git/config as a file, no git process). A file
| belongs to the known repo with the longest matching root. Each repo keeps
| its own claims, branch, supersede state and server session, opened the
| first time it has something to publish. The primary repo's state stays at
| the top of the session file, exactly where 1.2.0 kept it, so a session file
| written by 1.2.0 keeps working. A nested repo outside the allowlist is as
| inert as any other: nothing about it is sent or written.
|
| Every known repo is published, dirty files and all: they are on this
| machine, and showing them is Hive's job. But since 1.13.1 a teammate's hard
| collision wakes the agent only over a file this session's edit tools
| changed, and once; a collision on another repo's file, or on one changed
| before the session began, is said once as information, never as an order.
|
| Session start fetches the team picture for every known repo in ONE request,
| then starts a detached `publish` that publishes what is already uncommitted
| -- including edits made by hand before Claude Code opened -- without holding
| up the session. See docs/agent/workspaces.md.
|
|------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| VERBS
|------------------------------------------------------------------------------
|   session-start   SessionStart hook. The only blocking network call.
|   pre-edit        PreToolUse hook. ZERO network. Local cache only. p99 <40ms.
|   mark-dirty      PostToolUse hook, async. Sets a flag. ~5ms.
|   capture-plan    PostToolUse hook on ExitPlanMode, synchronous, ZERO
|                   network: the approved plan into the local spool, and one
|                   line for the agent when it is tied to no task (rule 1a).
|   capture-steps   PostToolUse hook on TodoWrite|TaskCreate|TaskUpdate,
|                   async, ZERO network: the live to-do steps into the spool,
|                   and the trailing push that sends them if no hook does.
|   resume-watch    Stop hook, async + asyncRewake: the idle session's watch
|                   on the files a parked task waits for; exit 2 wakes the
|                   agent once they are free. Returns at once with nothing
|                   parked, and in a headless run.
|   flush           PostToolBatch hook, asyncRewake. Debounced publish.
|   flush --final   Stop hook, async. Forced publish, ignores the debounce.
|                   Does nothing in a headless (`claude -p`) session, where
|                   Claude Code kills async hooks at exit...
|   flush --final --headless
|                   ...Stop hook, synchronous: the same publish, in a
|                   headless session only. Returns at once in any other.
|   session-end     SessionEnd hook, timeout 3. Final publish + close session.
|   publish         Not a hook: the detached run session-start and a
|                   multi-repo session-end start. Publishes every repo.
|   plan-sync       Not a hook: the detached push of one session's spool to
|                   PUT /progress/runs, started by flush, Stop and SessionEnd.
|                   With --trail, the trailing push: it sleeps until the
|                   debounce window closes, then sends what is still unsent.
|   plan-file       Not a hook: the detached capture of a plan file the agent
|                   wrote (rule 3a, SECTION 30), which mark-dirty starts once
|                   the file has gone HIVE_PLAN_FILE_DEBOUNCE seconds unwritten.
|   self-update     Not a hook: the detached run a plugin session start
|                   starts when Hive's policy names a newer client: at the
|                   first start after the news, then at most every six hours
|                   per computer, every one to six while the plugin is not
|                   published yet, once a day after two failures (SECTION
|                   41). Runs Claude Code's own `claude plugin marketplace
|                   update velu` and `claude plugin update velu-hive@velu`,
|                   nothing else, and sends nothing to Hive.
|   statusline      statusLine command. ZERO network. Ambient state, 0 tokens.
|   check           Not a hook: the agent runs it before a task's first
|                   edit with the files and folders it plans to change.
|                   One request per repository; prints who else has them
|                   open and exactly one HIVE NEXT line. `check --proceed`
|                   records the developer's answer, locally.
|   free            Not a hook: "what can I work on?" -- the folders nobody
|                   has uncommitted work in. Sends repository slugs only.
|   progress        Not a hook: the developer's progress report under
|                   <workspace>/.hive/progress-report, which this client
|                   builds and keeps. status | init | write | move | park |
|                   resume | next | take | sync | clean | restore. The agent
|                   runs them; the hooks start a detached `progress sync`
|                   when the tree changed. write and move also tie the
|                   agent's session to the task it works on, and take its
|                   estimate (--estimate). park puts a task on hold while a
|                   teammate's unpushed change holds its files, and resume
|                   brings it back once Hive says they are free (SECTION
|                   18e). next asks Hive what to work on next, and take
|                   makes an item of the owners' backlog the developer's
|                   task, with the owners' description of it shown to the
|                   agent inside <hive-brief>, as data (SECTION 18f). Since
|                   1.11.0 also plan, workplans, lanes, autopilot, takeover,
|                   ask, answer and inbox: workplans, lanes and the broker
|                   (SECTIONS 30 and 31). Since 1.12.0 also session, sessions
|                   and handover: the developer's own name for a session,
|                   their own live sessions, and a task handed from the
|                   session that holds it to another of theirs (SECTION 31);
|                   and `ask ... decision`, which asks a person through Hive
|                   (docs/api/decisions.md). Since 1.14.0 also say: the
|                   agent's one-line answer to its developer on Hive's board
|                   (SECTION 34; docs/api/rooms.md).
|   override CASE   Not a hook, and never pre-approved: the developer's own
|                   word against one of Hive's rulings (SECTION 33). Claude
|                   Code asks the developer before it runs.
|   doctor          Diagnostic table. The first thing support asks for.
|   usage on|off|status
|                   Not a hook: token usage on this computer (1.15.0,
|                   SECTION 40). off takes Hive's exporter settings out of
|                   the user settings and keeps them out; on writes them
|                   again; status says whether this computer sends usage.
|   usage-headers   Claude Code's otelHeadersHelper, never a hook and never
|                   the agent's: one JSON object of headers, the usage token
|                   or the off header, and exit 0, whatever happens.
|   audit           Last 20 outbound requests, so this is not a black box.
|   off / on        Per-machine kill switch. Survives restarts.
|   purge --yes     Uninstall: Hive's hooks, status line and rules out of the
|                   user settings, its section out of CLAUDE.local.md, and
|                   .hive/ and this folder deleted, with backups of every
|                   file of the developer's kept beside it. Revokes nothing:
|                   the owner does that in /hq.
|   setup CODE      One-paste install from /board -> My access: trades a
|                   single-use setup code (the argument, or HIVE_SETUP_CODE)
|                   for a token, then writes the credential, the hooks and
|                   CLAUDE.local.md. Never prints the token. Needs no
|                   restart: Claude Code loads the hooks into the session
|                   that ran it, and its next flush starts Hive there.
|                   Since 1.14.0 it installs the Velu Hive plugin for
|                   Claude Code instead, which updates itself, and makes
|                   ~/.claude/hive/hive.php a loader that runs the
|                   plugin's copy (SECTION 35); where it cannot, it keeps
|                   the settings hooks and says why. The session that ran
|                   it then needs /reload-plugins.
|
| Version and integrity: HIVE_VERSION below, and `hive.php doctor` prints this
| file's SHA-256 so it can be compared against the file the server serves
| (`curl -fsSL <HIVE_URL>/install/hive.php | sha256sum`). The file is never
| minified.
|
| PHP 8.1 syntax only. The server runs 8.5 but developers may be on older PHP,
| and a parse error in this file would break every hook on their machine.
|==============================================================================
*/

/*
|------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Web-SAPI guard -- MUST be the first executable statement in the file
|------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| This file is published at https://hive.velu.dev/install/hive.php so a
| developer can fetch it with one curl. But Laravel's public/.htaccess only
| rewrites to index.php when the request does NOT match a real file
| (`RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} !-f`), so a request for this path hits a
| real .php file and mod_php would EXECUTE it instead of serving it.
|
| Rather than fight that, lean into it: under any non-CLI SAPI, serve our own
| source as text and stop. The install URL then works exactly as intended, and
| none of the logic below is reachable over HTTP. The only thing exposed is this
| file, which is public by design: it is what a developer compares against.
*/
if (PHP_SAPI !== 'cli' && PHP_SAPI !== 'phpdbg') {
    if (! headers_sent()) {
        header('Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8');
        header('Content-Disposition: inline; filename="hive.php"');
        header('X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff');
        header('Cache-Control: public, max-age=300');
    }
    readfile(__FILE__);
    exit(0);
}

const HIVE_VERSION = '1.24.2';

/*
| Default base URL. Overridable with HIVE_URL, mostly so `doctor` can be pointed
| at a staging host or a deliberate black hole when rehearsing the fail-open
| path.
*/
const HIVE_DEFAULT_URL = 'https://hive.velu.dev';

/*
| Allowlist of repositories this client will talk about. ALLOWLIST, NOT DENYLIST:
| with a denylist, every new repository is published until somebody remembers to
| exclude it, and the first time that is noticed will be the day a client repo
| shows up on the board.
*/
const HIVE_DEFAULT_ALLOWLIST = 'github.com/velu-media/*';

/*
| Timeouts, in milliseconds, per verb. Connect is 800ms everywhere and that
| number is deliberate, not a guess: when the host is dead the failure arrives
| as a TCP SYN timeout, and Windows otherwise sits on a SYN for ~21 seconds. An
| 800ms ceiling converts "Claude Code froze" into "Hive is offline".
|
| Read timeouts differ because the blocking budget differs: session-start is
| inside a ~1.5s SessionStart budget, flush runs async so it can afford 3s, and
| SessionEnd hooks share a 1.5s budget across all of them unless one asks for
| longer -- so session-end stays under 1.2s even though our hook asks for 3.
*/
const HIVE_TIMEOUTS = [
    'session-start' => [800, 1500],
    'flush' => [800, 3000],
    'session-end' => [600, 1200],
    'doctor' => [1500, 4000],
    // `check` and `free`: the agent's Bash tool waits on them, a question at
    // a time, never inside a hook budget. One read per repository at most.
    'check' => [800, 3000],
    // A human waiting on a one-off command, not a hook inside a budget. The
    // redeem registers the machine and mints a token, so it gets more room.
    'setup' => [3000, 10000],
    // `progress`: the agent's Bash tool, or a detached sync. Each verb has a
    // wall-clock budget of its own on top (HIVE_PROGRESS_*_BUDGET).
    'progress' => [800, 3000],
    // `verb` (hunt 7, Task 20): the request bind, take, write or move is run
    // for (hive_http_verb()). The agent's own Bash call, which waits for its
    // answer anyway, never a hook: HIVE_PROGRESS_VERB_BUDGET holds the whole
    // verb, and a read gets what is left of it. On 16-17 October a 3 s read
    // printed "Hive did not answer in time" for a bind and a take whose
    // transaction had committed.
    'verb' => [800, 10000],
    // A change of state said first, before the flush's lock and publish
    // (hive_activity_send_first()): HIVE_ACTIVITY_STATE_FIRST in all, split
    // so that an answer has time to come. Cut from the flush's pair, a
    // deadline that short would leave 1 ms to read. The connection gets the
    // larger part: DNS, TCP and TLS take two round trips and more, and a
    // developer 200 ms from Hive must not fail every first send, each a
    // breaker failure; cURL's whole request is held to the 800 ms all the
    // same. One that fails is said again by the flush's heartbeat or its
    // last act.
    'state-first' => [600, 200],
    // A plan document over 256 KB on a detached path (the `plan-file` run, its
    // spool retry, the workspace sync) or inside a push's own deadline:
    // hive_plan_upload_window() holds the request to 3 s and 1 s per started
    // 256 KB, at most this pair's 15 s, and never past the run's own deadline.
    // Its upload is most of it: a 2 MiB plan takes 3.4 s at 5 Mbit/s.
    'plan-upload' => [800, 14200],
];

/*
| The ONLY fields ever taken out of hook stdin. Everything else -- old_string,
| new_string, content, command, prompt, transcript_path -- is discarded
| unexamined, but for what rules 1a and 1b of the header name. Nested reads
| are spelled out explicitly rather than walked generically, because a
| generic walker is one refactor away from leaking.
*/
const HOOK_INPUT_ALLOWLIST = [
    'session_id',
    'cwd',
    'hook_event_name',
    'source',           // SessionStart: startup | resume | clear | compact | fork
    'reason',           // SessionEnd:   clear | resume | logout | prompt_input_exit | other
    'tool_name',
    'permission_mode',
